{"parentUuid":"02a8f81e-1a69-4a33-b6f5-c8b2d9c1ad4a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CffzMXcxb87vo3wbekwBi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BDZKLA4y3YUbvHkotGXwhy","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"data","summary":"Approved: finish all six, swappable paths","message":"Approved — finish all six in one pass, including the SVGs. Do not skip them.\n\ne036/e037 are beat 5, the counterfeit catch, and the two-images-side-by-side shot is the single most important frame in the demo — it is what wins X-Factor. Falling back to a findings[] table turns the climax into a spreadsheet. Ten minutes is cheap for that.\n\nOne constraint on the SVGs: **make the paths trivially swappable for real photographs.** The user is photographing an actual DIMM (one genuine, one with an altered label), and if those land before the 16:30 freeze we want to drop them in without touching the event log. Keep the filenames stable and obvious, and note the swap procedure in status/data.md so it is a copy, not a code change. Hand-written SVG is fine as the fallback — a clean schematic label may actually read better on a projector than a phone photo under venue lighting, so this is not a compromise.\n\nWrite status/data.md as part of the pass; it is the only track status file still missing.\n\nYour two design decisions are both right and both improve the demo:\n\nThe counterfeit candidate carrying the qualified incumbent MPN is the sharper construction. QVL answers \"was this tested here\" and cannot answer \"is this genuine\" — keeping those as two independent gates is truthful and it means beats 4 and 5 each justify their own existence rather than being one idea told twice. Good instinct warning AGENTS not to let their engine clear it.\n\nBeat 4's strong version is the right call for the same reason. A part that matches all five requirements field-for-field and is still blocked is the actual engineering position, and it is far more interesting on stage than a part that fails a spec — anyone can see why a mismatched part gets rejected. \"Electrically fine, cheapest by $209,203, never tested in this board\" is the whole argument for why procurement cannot be automated naively, which is our thesis.\n\nAgreed on `tool: \"qvl\"` over `\"zoodata\"` in e023 — you extended the reasoning correctly. We do not claim sponsor integrations we are not making.\n\nAlso: the rollup you surfaced is the best line anyone has produced today. **Memory is 2.06% of node material cost and 100% of the stoppage — a $214 stick blocks a $249,561 node.** Put that in status/data.md flagged for the presenter. It is going in the pitch.","type":"message","recipient":"data","content":"Approved — finish all six in one pass, including …"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2468,"cache_read_input_tokens":180426,"output_tokens":1404,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":483},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2468,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1404,"cache_read_input_tokens":180426,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2468,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2468},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BDZKLA4y3YUbvHkotGXwhy":{"to":"data","summary":"Approved: finish all six, swappable paths","message":"Approved — finish all six in one pass, including the SVGs. Do not skip them.\n\ne036/e037 are beat 5, the counterfeit catch, and the two-images-side-by-side shot is the single most important frame in the demo — it is what wins X-Factor. Falling back to a findings[] table turns the climax into a spreadsheet. Ten minutes is cheap for that.\n\nOne constraint on the SVGs: **make the paths trivially swappable for real photographs.** The user is photographing an actual DIMM (one genuine, one with an altered label), and if those land before the 16:30 freeze we want to drop them in without touching the event log. Keep the filenames stable and obvious, and note the swap procedure in status/data.md so it is a copy, not a code change. Hand-written SVG is fine as the fallback — a clean schematic label may actually read better on a projector than a phone photo under venue lighting, so this is not a compromise.\n\nWrite status/data.md as part of the pass; it is the only track status file still missing.\n\nYour two design decisions are both right and both improve the demo:\n\nThe counterfeit candidate carrying the qualified incumbent MPN is the sharper construction. QVL answers \"was this tested here\" and cannot answer \"is this genuine\" — keeping those as two independent gates is truthful and it means beats 4 and 5 each justify their own existence rather than being one idea told twice. Good instinct warning AGENTS not to let their engine clear it.\n\nBeat 4's strong version is the right call for the same reason. A part that matches all five requirements field-for-field and is still blocked is the actual engineering position, and it is far more interesting on stage than a part that fails a spec — anyone can see why a mismatched part gets rejected. \"Electrically fine, cheapest by $209,203, never tested in this board\" is the whole argument for why procurement cannot be automated naively, which is our thesis.\n\nAgreed on `tool: \"qvl\"` over `\"zoodata\"` in e023 — you extended the reasoning correctly. We do not claim sponsor integrations we are not making.\n\nAlso: the rollup you surfaced is the best line anyone has produced today. **Memory is 2.06% of node material cost and 100% of the stoppage — a $214 stick blocks a $249,561 node.** Put that i