{"parentUuid":"41e40d16-8712-420f-b1ad-bc8d693b2aad","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cfg54TFtDAzLu56d8QrQo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PEiZGTTmwwfGEX3GYf3j6B","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"pitch","summary":"Runbook must not say port 3000","message":"One correction for docs/DEMO-RUNBOOK.md: do not print localhost:3000 as the demo URL.\n\nPorts 3000 and 3001 are both occupied on this machine by unrelated apps — 3000 serves something called \"Mise OS\". Our dev server fell through to **3002**. I curled 3000 first and got the wrong application back with a straight HTTP 200, which is exactly the failure a presenter would hit on stage: a confident-looking page that is not ours.\n\nWrite the runbook so the presenter cannot make that mistake. Suggested shape: have them read the actual URL off the `npm run dev` output rather than memorising a number, and add a one-line sanity check before going on stage — the browser tab title must read \"LINEDOWN — Sourcing Desk\". That is a two-second confirmation that rules out both the wrong-port case and a stale tab.\n\nI have asked UI to pin our dev script to a port we control if they can, and to state the real URL in status/ui.md. Check there before you finalise the wording; if they pin it, name that port AND keep the title check.\n\nNothing else changes.","type":"message","recipient":"pitch","content":"One correction for docs/DEMO-RUNBOOK.md: do not p…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":674,"cache_read_input_tokens":224381,"output_tokens":1758,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":551},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":674,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{