{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-10-03T22:28:58.068Z","sessionId":"79c3b8a7-06a2-4fb1-8964-a4968e4c0492","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a0e386646fb2e3e81</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-abhijitbetigeri/79c3b8a7-06a2-4fb1-8964-a4968e4c0492/tasks/a0e386646fb2e3e81.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Presentation script and stage notes\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Found and removed two false claims — both were in my own draft, and neither would have survived a judge's follow-up.\n\n## 1. \"The run is halted inside ZooWork's runtime\" — false, struck\n\nAGENTS confirmed **no ZooWork API call was ever made**: no key in this environment, so no agent, no session, no `agent.custom_tool_use`, no `resolveCustomToolCall`. The orchestrator calls the tool handlers directly in process.\n\nBeat 4's narration now reads: *\"That block isn't a model being cautious — it's a function of the data. Put that part on the qualified list and the same code clears it.\"* That's **stronger** than what it replaced, because it's checkable — `agents/` is 25/25 green and one test performs exactly that verdict flip. Q2 is now a two-layer answer that gives the gate first (ours, exercised, data-driven) then volunteers the limit outright, and offers the test suite as the thing a judge *can* verify. The §5 ZooWork line is restated as architecture and labelled as such.\n\n**Strategic flag for you:** this materially weakens the Best-Use-of-ZooWork claim. If anyone can get a key and make one real `createAgent` + `resolveCustomToolCall` round trip before 18:00, that single call is worth more to the named prize than any other remaining work. Your call — I'm not reopening the freeze, and the pitch is honest as it stands.\n\n## 2. \"`entire why` prints the actual prompt\" — not demonstrable, struck\n\nI tested it rather than trusting it. `entire why data/qvl.json:12` — the command my own runbook told the presenter to pre-open — **fails**, because that path isn't in HEAD. On the committed `data/cache/qvl-rev-E.proposed.json` it runs, resolves line and author, then prints *\"No Entire checkpoint is linked to the commit that last touched this line.\"* The capability is real and the hook is installed, but nothing is linked to our commits.\n\nBeat 7 now claims the **committed decision record**, which is real and showable: commit `dab5f15`, message *\"QVL rev-E: Samsung M321R8GA0BB0-CQK qualified primary alternate; TRA564G48D436O blocked pending evaluation; broker lot PRC-24817 blacklisted.\"* Runbook default is now **no terminal**; if one is shown it's `git log -1`, which cannot fail.\n\n## Also corrected\n\n- **Runbook's reason for excluding the live run was stale.** AGENTS fixed it — beat 5 emits, `plain` is 44/44, with a `validate()` guard. The accurate reason is that it hasn't been rehearsed, not that it's incomplete. A judge may ask, so it's worth being right.\n- **Q12 now carries AGENTS' BAND evidence** — the five event strings, zero hits for critic/veto/gate — framed as a design judgement made on evidence rather than a gap.\n\nBoth deliverables now claim only what's on disk or exercised in a test. The pattern across all four corrections tonight is the same: every figure or capability I checked myself held up, and every one I inherited from a summary did not.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>151883</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>37</tool_uses><duration_ms>981609</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","reason":"absorbed_mid_turn"}
